= Biological database =

== Overview ==
A '''biological database''' is an organized, queryable collection of biological data and associated metadata designed for storage, retrieval, integration, curation, or analysis.

Biological databases can store nucleotide or protein sequences, structures, variants, expression measurements, pathways, taxonomy, literature links, phenotypes, interactions, or many other data types.

Some resources are archival/primary repositories; others curate, integrate, or derive information from multiple sources. Database entries can therefore mix submitted observations, computed annotations, expert curation, identifiers, and cross-references.

A database is not merely a static file collection. Schema, identifiers, versions, provenance, update policy, access interfaces, curation rules, and interoperability strongly affect scientific use.

== Boundaries ==
* biological database ≠ flat file collection
* database record ≠ experimentally verified truth
* primary archive ≠ curated derived knowledge by identity

== Related concepts ==
[[Database]], [[Metadata]], [[Provenance]], [[Biological sequence]], [[Sequence annotation]], [[Genomics]], and [[Bioinformatics]] define the data-resource context.

== Archives, curation and predictions ==
An archival repository receives records from submitters and maintains retrievable identifiers and metadata. A curated resource can reconcile records, evaluate published evidence and add expert interpretation. A computationally annotated collection adds predictions or classifications that may not have been manually verified. These functions can coexist, but their evidence levels should not be conflated.

For example, NCBI GenBank primarily collects submitted nucleotide sequence data; submitted records can later be updated. UniProtKB distinguishes reviewed Swiss-Prot records from unreviewed TrEMBL records containing computational analysis. Neither a stable identifier nor a “reviewed” label proves every individual claim true without examining its evidence and provenance.

== Identifier and version lineage ==
A reproducible record-level reference should identify the database, accession, version or release where applicable, and retrieval date when records evolve. Sequence versioning, cross-database mapping and taxonomic names can change. A publication may cite a reference sequence that has since been updated, so exact record state matters.

== Connections to genomic interpretation ==
[[Related to::Genomics|Genomics]] uses sequence repositories and reference annotations for comparison and discovery. Database integration can expose useful correspondences, but mapping two accessions to the same entity needs explicit identity criteria. Contradictory annotations should be flagged and traced to their source rather than silently overwritten.

